@page "/how-it-works"
@* For a client's people, not the operator: the workflow in plain words, walked through with the
   Beauty Bank email Neelam actually sent. It reads nothing from storage -- the email is the
   library's sample (BeautyBankEmail) and the checks run here -- so it is open to anyone, and its
   layout carries no admin links. Open means protected only by obscurity: accepted by the owner on
   2026-10-09 for now, since the email already went to Neelam's customers. *@
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>How it works</PageTitle>

<h1>How a campaign gets from idea to inbox</h1>

<p>
    Square Marketing sends your emails, but it cannot check them. So the email is written here
    first, checked, and only then copied into Square, block by block, ready to send. Below, each
    step uses the Beauty Bank email you sent for your first anniversary.
</p>

<section class="card" aria-labelledby="step-template">
    <h2 id="step-template">1. Start from a template</h2>
    <p>
        A template is the shape of one kind of email: its parts, in the order you send them. The parts
        that never change, such as your sign-off, are written once in the template. A new kind of
        email is a new template, and your templates are yours.
    </p>
    <p>
        Here is your template, worked out from the Beauty Bank email:
        <strong>@Template.Name</strong>. Five parts are written once and go out the same every time;
        the rest you write for each campaign.
    </p>
    <ol class="shape">
        @foreach (var block in Template.Blocks.Where(b => b.Type != BlockType.Spacer))
        {
            <li>
                <strong>@block.Label</strong>
                <span class="muted">@(block.Fixed is not null ? "written once" : "you write it each time")@(block.Required ? "" : ", optional")</span>
            </li>
        }
    </ol>
    <p>And what any email from it looks like, with the parts you fill in marked:</p>
    <EmailPreview Blocks="TemplateBlocks" />
    <p class="muted">Ready to try now: <a href="templates">Templates</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="card" aria-labelledby="step-write">
    <h2 id="step-write">2. Write the campaign</h2>
    <p>
        Each campaign starts from a template, with everything that changes left empty, never last
        time's words. When you copy an offer tier to save typing, the copy is marked as not yet read:
        the campaign will not go further until you have changed or confirmed each copied line and
        given the new tier its own name and price.
    </p>
    <p>This is the Beauty Bank email as it went out the second time:</p>
    <EmailPreview Blocks="SentBlocks" />
    <p class="muted">Try it: <a href="campaigns">Campaigns</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="card" aria-labelledby="step-check">
    <h2 id="step-check">3. Check it, twice</h2>
    <p>
        First, instant rules catch what can be stated exactly. What they must stop has to be fixed;
        the rest is worth a look. Here is what they find in the email that went out:
    </p>
    <ul class="findings">
        @foreach (var finding in SentReview.Findings.OrderByDescending(f => f.Severity))
        {
            <li class="finding @(finding.Severity == Severity.Blocker ? "blocker" : "warning")">
                <strong>@(finding.Severity == Severity.Blocker ? "Must fix" : "Worth a look")</strong>
                <span class="muted">@finding.Location</span>
                <span>@finding.Message</span>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
    <p>
        The first send, 38 minutes earlier, offered the $299 option twice under the same name. The
        rules stop that too: @FirstSendBlockers thing@(FirstSendBlockers == 1 ? "" : "s") to fix before it could go.
    </p>
    <p>
        Then an AI proofreader reads it the way a careful editor would: spelling, prices that do not
        match, contradictions, placeholder text left in. If it is wrong about something, you can
        dismiss that one finding, with your name and a reason, which are kept. If the proofread cannot
        run at all, the campaign waits rather than going out unread.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">The rules above are real and run as this page loads. The AI proofread is not switched on yet.</p>
</section>

<section class="card" aria-labelledby="step-approve">
    <h2 id="step-approve">4. Approve</h2>
    <p>
        A person signs off on the checked campaign before it can leave. Nothing is exported while
        anything is still blocking it.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">Being built.</p>
</section>

<section class="card" aria-labelledby="step-send">
    <h2 id="step-send">5. Copy it into Square and send</h2>
    <p>
        The approved email comes out as a short list of blocks, each with a copy button. Paste them
        into Square's editor in order, and the email looks the way you designed it. Then send it from
        Square as you do today.
    </p>
    <p>
        One way the Beauty Bank email could look with everything fixed: the lower option renamed Gold
        Member, a disclaimer, a terms link, and a button to join. The join and terms links are
        placeholders, since no page for joining existed. These are the blocks you would paste:
    </p>
    <EmailPreview Blocks="CorrectedBlocks" />
    @if (CorrectedReview.Blockers.Any())
    {
        <p>Still to fix: @CorrectedReview.Blockers.Count().</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>
            Nothing left that the rules must stop@(CorrectedReview.Warnings.Any()
                ? $"; {CorrectedReview.Warnings.Count()} worth a look, such as the offer's name, which is yours to decide"
                : "").
        </p>
    }
    <p class="muted">Being built.</p>
</section>

@code {
    // Static: the sample and the rules are the same for every visitor, so they are worked out once.
    private static readonly CampaignTemplate Template = BeautyBankEmail.MembershipTemplate();
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<EditorBlock> TemplateBlocks = TemplatePreview.Blocks(Template.Blocks);
    private static readonly Campaign Sent = BeautyBankEmail.SecondSend();
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<EditorBlock> SentBlocks = EditorExport.PreviewBlocks(Sent);
    private static readonly ReviewReport SentReview = CampaignReview.Check(Sent);
    private static readonly int FirstSendBlockers = CampaignReview.Check(BeautyBankEmail.FirstSend()).Blockers.Count();
    private static readonly Campaign Corrected = BeautyBankEmail.Corrected();
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<EditorBlock> CorrectedBlocks = EditorExport.PreviewBlocks(Corrected);
    private static readonly ReviewReport CorrectedReview = CampaignReview.Check(Corrected);
}
